<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albarracín</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albarracín is a small walled town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a>, Aragon, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on a rock above the Guadalaviar river. In the 11th century it was the seat of the Banu Razin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a>, and from 1170 it was an independent Christian lordship of the Azagra family until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a> conquered it in 1284. Its medieval walls and houses make it one of Spain's best-preserved historic towns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albarracin.jpg" alt="Albarracín" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albarrac%C3%ADn,_Teruel,_Espa%C3%B1a,_2014-01-10,_DD_120.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lobetani are recorded mainly by Ptolemy in the 2nd century CE, who places them in the mountains of eastern Spain with a town called Lobeton, perhaps near <b>Albarracín</b> in modern Teruel.</blockquote><a href="/state/lobetani">Lobetani</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1170 the Navarrese noble Pedro Ruiz de Azagra obtained <b>Albarracín</b> from Ibn Mardanish of the Second Taifa of Murcia, whom he had served.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-albarracin">Lordship of Albarracín</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Albarracín</b> was a rugged frontier lordship of castles, pasture and timber between the taifas of Toledo, Zaragoza and Valencia.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-albarracin">Taifa of Albarracín</a></li><li><blockquote>Molina was a small border principality between the taifas of Toledo, Zaragoza and <b>Albarracín</b>, more a fortified lordship than a full kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-molina">Taifa of Molina</a></li><li><blockquote>By about 1092 Ibn Labbun had handed the town to the ruler of <b>Albarracín</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-murviedro">Taifa of Murviedro</a></li></ul></section>
